n_name": "methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "N2", "iupac_name": "dinitrogen", "common_name": "nitrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/N2/c1-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Erythrose-producing branch of glycolaldehyde self-aldol chemistry", "inputs": ["mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ions, providing general-base catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340 K, proposed operating range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Alkaline concentrated water, approximately pH 9-11. Erythrose is one product of a mixture containing other tetroses and further condensation products. Subsequent acidification is required."}, "mechanism": "Base-mediated enolization and aldol addition combine glycolaldehyde molecules into tetroses. This node represents the erythrose-producing branch, not selective conversion of the entire feed.", "reasoning": "Formose-derived tetroses are identified as possible erythrose sources in the supplied discussion of Shen et al. (1987) and V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018). Glycolaldehyde self-aldol chemistry provides the required carbon skeleton without an oxidant. However, the supplied literature does not demonstrate selective erythrose accumulation followed by end-to-end histidine synthesis. The proposed environmental integration is speculative; competing threose formation, degradation, and further oligomerization are major limitations."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Candidate formamidine supply by HCN ammoniation", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K, exploratory rather than validated", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated ammonia/HCN microenvironments with limited volatilization; rapid downstream consumption would be necessary. No productive rate or yield is assigned."}, "mechanism": "Proposed nucleophilic addition of ammonia to HCN followed by proton transfers gives formamidine. This represents a candidate supply reaction, not an established efficient aqueous process.", "reasoning": "Speculative precursor-supply edge. Shen et al. (1987, 1990) considered HCN/ammonia-derived formamidine prebiotically relevant, but useful production from dilute mixtures was problematic. V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018) emphasized competing formamidine hydrolysis. The edge closes the nitrogenous precursor inventory chemically but remains a principal feasibility bottleneck; it must not be interpreted as demonstrated accumulation of approximately 30 mM formamidine."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Observed erythrose\u2013formamidine assembly of imidazole acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately 0.03 M erythrose and 0.03 M formamidine, pH approximately 6, about 12 hours. Concentration and pH must be staged separately from alkaline tetrose formation."}, "mechanism": "Coarse-grained condensation, rearrangement, cyclization, and dehydration construct the substituted imidazole. The endpoint is established, but the microscopic order of these events is not uniquely resolved.", "reasoning": "Shen et al. (1987) identified imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, with a maximum reported yield of 1.6% relative to erythrose, without an added mineral catalyst. Shen et al. (1990) subsequently obtained histidine from the precursor-generating mixture. The original precursor concentration was 0.03 M, not the 0.3 M quoted in V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018). Availability and survival of both precursors remain unresolved geochemical constraints."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Observed erythrose\u2013formamidine formation of imidazole glycol", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately 0.03 M precursor concentrations, mildly acidic water near pH 6, approximately 12 hours."}, "mechanism": "Condensation and ring closure retain a vicinal-diol side chain, producing imidazole-4-glycol with net water elimination.", "reasoning": "Shen et al. (1987) reported imidazole-4-glycol at up to 6.8% yield relative to erythrose. Its formation is experimentally supported, but its quantitative conversion into the histidine precursor aldehyde was not established as a separately closed pathway. It is therefore represented as an observed potential reservoir rather than an automatically productive histidine intermediate."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Proposed acid-promoted conversion of imidazole glycol to aldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions, providing acid catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370 K, proposed range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Separate acidic stage, approximately pH 2-5. Product distribution and residence time require measurement before downstream neutralization."}, "mechanism": "Proposed pinacol-type dehydration and hydride migration convert the heteroaryl vicinal diol into the side-chain aldehyde. Competing rearrangements may produce other carbonyl compounds.", "reasoning": "Speculative completion of an experimentally observed precursor branch. Shen et al. (1987) discussed glycol-to-aldehyde dehydration as plausible, but the supplied research does not establish a selective, geochemically validated conversion. The proposed hydride-migration orientation gives the required aldehyde without molecular oxygen; its regioselectivity, yield, and rate are unmeasured bottlenecks."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Proposed anoxic redox closure of the erythrose\u2013formaldehyde\u2013ammonia glycol branch", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "353 K as an experimental reference, not validation of the proposed redox mechanism", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated erythrose, formaldehyde, and ammonium/ammonia near pH 6.6. Additional formaldehyde supplies a proposed sacrificial oxidant and is reduced to methanol. Molecular oxygen is excluded."}, "mechanism": "Coarse-grained carbonyl\u2013ammonia condensation constructs the imidazole glycol while a proposed coupled transfer of reducing equivalents converts formaldehyde to methanol.", "reasoning": "Shen et al. (1987) observed imidazole-4-glycol in erythrose/formaldehyde/ammonia mixtures, at approximately 1.8% maximum yield, but did not identify the necessary oxidant. Formaldehyde was among the possibilities considered. This oxygen-free closure is speculative: methanol is a predicted balancing coproduct, not a reported product assignment for this mechanism. Neither the electron-transfer mechanism nor its rate under these conditions is established."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hypothesized precyclization dehydration of erythrose", "inputs": ["mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions, providing acid catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370 K, exploratory range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 2-5 in a separate