stions\": [\n        \"Record the reported 80 C water reservoir, seven-day exposure, gas replenishment and aqueous collection/work-up within the endpoint definition.\",\n        \"Keep hydrogen production explicitly identified as formal closure, without treating its coefficient as a measured histidine-specific yield.\",\n        \"Obtain absolute histidine yields and target-focused replication, and justify reduced-gas supply and retention in a terrestrial setting.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.85\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.85,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.86,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.83,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.81,\n      \"feedback\": \"Criado-Reyes et al. (2021) reported histidine in this buffered reactor configuration; the buffered configurations collectively yielded approximately 0.08-0.12 mg per experiment. The empirical endpoint representation is appropriate. Histidine identification relied on derivatized GC-MS library matching rather than the paper's authentic-standard co-injection subset, which limits certainty without invalidating the assignment. Borosilicate walls are reported apparatus conditions, not evidence that a specific natural mineral catalyst is required.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Encode pH approximately 8.7, 0.05 M NH4Cl, reported gas partial pressures and the discharge schedule as protocol conditions.\",\n        \"Confirm histidine with an authentic standard and orthogonal analysis, and distinguish laboratory vessel effects from environmental implementation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.85\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.85,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.86,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.83,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.81,\n      \"feedback\": \"The buffered Teflon-only protocol is directly represented in Criado-Reyes et al. (2021), supporting histidine production without borosilicate contact. Teflon should be understood as an inert experimental boundary, not a required prebiotic material. The endpoint remains mechanistically unresolved, and histidine-specific identification has the same library-matching limitation as the other configurations. Its score is not increased or decreased because glass is absent.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Supply the buffered protocol's quantitative solution, gas and discharge conditions and define the aqueous processing included in the endpoint.\",\n        \"Preserve the no-required-solid-catalyst interpretation while validating histidine identity, yield and production under environmentally justified gas-supply conditions.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.85\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.85,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.86,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.83,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.81,\n      \"feedback\": \"The buffered Teflon-plus-borosilicate-pieces experiment is supported by Criado-Reyes et al. (2021). It is correctly treated as an operational variant, not a separately established histidine mechanism. The experiment supports a histidine endpoint but does not identify glass-mediated elementary steps or establish a histidine-specific catalytic requirement. Quantitative operating conditions and the analytical confirmation limitation should accompany the reaction record.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Document