021), reinforcing that glass is unnecessary for the reported histidine signal. It is a valid coarse-grained protocol representation, subject to the same unresolved mechanism and histidine-confirmation limitations as the other configurations. Neither a fixed alkaline environment nor a naturally sustained reduced-gas inventory follows from the initial laboratory setup.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_015\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify pH evolution, gas-liquid exchange, discharge exposure and the processing boundary associated with the measured product.\",\n        \"Retain Teflon as an inert control descriptor, and test histidine production under quantitatively justified terrestrial feedstock and residence-time conditions.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.85\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.85,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.86,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.83,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.81,\n      \"feedback\": \"The initially alkaline Teflon-plus-glass-pieces experiment is supported by Criado-Reyes et al. (2021). Its inclusion accurately represents an observed protocol variant, but contributes no independently resolved histidine pathway. The network appropriately avoids assigning an unobserved Strecker or hydantoin mechanism. Remaining limitations concern quantitative environmental conditions, changing pH, target confirmation and whether reactor-contact effects transfer to a natural setting.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Document glass exposure, pH history, gas pressures and discharge schedule without interpreting initial alkalinity as a maintained condition.\",\n        \"Confirm histidine using an authentic standard and independent analytical method, then test any proposed natural-surface analogue with matched controls.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.8,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"The network is structurally sound: 15 consistently used molecule nodes and 16 reaction nodes form a connected graph, and histidine is reachable from the listed starting materials. Accepting the supplied deterministic classifications, all reactions are balanced or balanceable; none is penalized for conservation failure. Formal endpoint balancing does not establish measured hydrogen yields or a selective discharge stoichiometry. Literature alignment is strong at the strategy level: the network retains the experimentally supported Shen core, represents discharge observations as empirical source terms, acknowledges precursor challenges, and avoids unsupported target-producing branches. Reaction-local citations and quantitative conditions are nevertheless largely absent. The principal aqueous-network gaps are productive erythrose supply, formamidine persistence, pH-stage compatibility and validated hydrolysis timescales. The discharge protocols support target production, but do not resolve histidine mechanisms, natural operating conditions or all analytical uncertainties. P4 has the strongest complete listed protocol evidence, while the Shen core has better-resolved molecular chemistry. The six Criado-Reyes variants are not counted as independent mechanistic confirmations; pathway, hub and environment counts do not affect the assessment. Flagged reactions require operating-condition or evidentiary clarification, not necessarily correction of chemically invalid transformations. Overall, this is a credible conditional and empirical synthesis network, but it does not meet the 0.90 network approval threshold.\"\n}", "parsed_response": {"pathway_evaluations": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_002": 0.74, "rxn_003": 0.94, "rxn_004": 0.91, "rxn_005": 0.93, "rxn_006": 0.8, "rxn