xn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Buffered borosilicate-contact discharge protocol\",\n      \"description\": \"Criado-Reyes et al. (2021), buffered borosilicate-reactor configuration. Distinct experimental surface conditions do not establish a distinct histidine mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Buffered mixed-wall discharge protocol\",\n      \"description\": \"Criado-Reyes et al. (2021), buffered Teflon reactor containing borosilicate pieces. An operational surface-contact variant of the same unresolved discharge family.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Initially alkaline mineral-independent discharge protocol\",\n      \"description\": \"Criado-Reyes et al. (2021), unbuffered Teflon-only configuration. Initial alkalinity distinguishes the protocol, but a separate molecular pathway has not been established.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Initially alkaline borosilicate-contact discharge protocol\",\n      \"description\": \"Criado-Reyes et al. (2021), unbuffered borosilicate-reactor configuration. Histidine is a reported analytical endpoint; glass-specific target catalysis is not demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Initially alkaline mixed-wall discharge protocol\",\n      \"description\": \"Criado-Reyes et al. (2021), unbuffered Teflon reactor with borosilicate pieces. Shares mechanistic and analytical uncertainties with P4-P8 and is not independent confirmation of another synthesis family.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-containing seven-day discharge protocol\",\n      \"description\": \"Jiang et al. (2014), methane-ammonia-hydrogen-water discharge with replenished gases. Histidine is included in the reported amino-acid inventory, but no target-specific intermediate sequence or absolute histidine yield is established in the supplied record.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_013\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Histidine", "overall_strategy": "Construct an evidence-qualified network containing ten distinct graph or experimental-protocol pathways, not ten independently established histidine-forming mechanisms. P1 is the experimentally supported, precursor-fed Shen synthesis. P2 and P3 are conditional upstream extensions through glycolaldehyde aldol and formose chemistry. P4-P9 represent the six reported reactor/pH configurations of Criado-Reyes et al. (2021); P10 represents Jiang et al. (2014). These seven discharge pathways are empirical source terms, not resolved molecular mechanisms or seven independent reaction families. Erythrose can be supplied initially or generated through the optional upstream reactions; formamidine is a conditional external feedstock because productive geochemical synthesis and persistence are unresolved. All routes use anoxic surface gas-water systems or staged aqueous compartments. Alkaline sugar production, mildly acidic imidazole assembly, cyanide capture, and acidic hydrolysis are not assumed to occur simultaneously in one pond. Proposed operating windows are distinguished from reported experimental parameters, and missing concentrations or residence times are not invented. The 15 K ice experiments are outside the stipulated terrestrial environments; hot dry CO/H2/NH3 catalysis is not relabeled as demonstrated aqueous hydrothermal synthesis. Molecules use neutral reference formulas with pH-dependent protonation omitted. Discharge reactions are atom-balanceable selected-product projections: hydrogen bookkeeping does not establish measured H2 production, and nitrogen provenance remains unresolved. The network is structurally complete for these selected pathways but is not a fully validated end-to-end geochemical synthesis. No enantiomeric enrichment is assumed.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "glycolaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "C4H8O4", "iupac_name": "(2R,3R)-2,3,4-trihydroxybutanal", "common_name": "D-erythrose", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "CH4N2", "iupac_name": "methanimidamide", "common_name": "formamidine", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethanal", "common_name": "imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C6H8N4", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanenitrile", "common_name": "histidine aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C6H9N3O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid", "common_name": "histidine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H9N3O2/c7-4(6(10)11)1-5-2-8-3-9-5/h2-4H,1,7H2,(H,8,9)(H,10,11)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "N2", "iupac_name": "dinitrogen", "common_name": "nitrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/N2/c1-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Conditional erythrose supply by glycolaldehyde self-aldol chemistry", "inputs": ["mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ions: solution-base catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K; proposed screening window, not a reported histidine-network protocol", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 9-11 in alkaline, concentrating water. Effective glycolaldehyde concentration, residence time, erythrose yield, and survival time are not established by the supplied research. The selected product node represents the D-erythrose fraction of an expected stereoisomeric mixture, not selective D-erythrose production. Transfer to the imidazole stage requires quenching alkaline chemistry and demonstrating an approximately 30 mM erythrose inventory."}, "mechanism": "Base-catalyzed enolization and intermolecular aldol addition combine glycolaldehyde units into aldotetroses. Erythrose is one product alongside other stereoisomers and competing products.", "reasoning": "Speculative as an integrated feedstock-supply step, although the aldol reaction class is established. Shen et al. (1987) used supplied erythrose rather than demonstrating this upstream production-and-transfer sequence. V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018) identify tetrose scarcity and instability as major limitations. Neither citation establishes selective, persistent erythrose accumulation under the proposed conditions. This edge therefore cannot inherit the experimental confidence or histidine yield of the downstream Shen synthesis."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Conditional formaldehyde-fed formose branch to erythrose", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved calcium ions", "Hydroxide ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K; proposed alkaline formose window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Calcium-assisted solution-base catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 10-11 in a calcium-bearing concentrating pool. Glycolaldehyde supplies a C2 entry substrate; formaldehyde supplies additional carbon. Productive feed concentrations and residence time are not established for coupling to histidine synthesis. Terminate alkaline aging before extensive sugar degradation, then change to approximately pH 6. No experimentally demonstrated concentration or selection process delivering 30 mM erythrose is assumed."}, "mechanism": "A coarse-grained formose branch incorporates C1 units through aldol additions and carbonyl isomerizations to produce a tetrose mixture containing erythrose. It is not an elementary or selective formaldehyde-to-erythrose reaction.", "reasoning": "Speculative as a completed upstream connection. Formose chemistry is discussed as a possible erythrose source in the Shen framework, but the supplied studies do not demonstrate crude formose mixture to histidine conversion (Shen et al., 1987; Shen et al., 1990). The instability and low selectivity emphasized by V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018) remain unresolved. Calcium and hydroxide are included for their established roles in formose chemistry, not as histidine-specific catalysts."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Experimentally supported erythrose-formamidine assembly of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported precursor experiment: 0.03 M D-erythrose and 0.03 M formamidine, approximately pH 6.0, heated for 12 h. No added mineral catalyst. A terrestrial implementation requires replenishment or transient concentration of both precursors, protection against destructive surface irradiation, and prompt downstream use. The experimental concentrations were 0.03 M, not 0.3 M."}, "mechanism": "Net condensation, rearrangement, and dehydration assemble the substituted imidazole ring. An Amadori-like sequence is proposed; the exact microscopic order is not established. Water is the formal condensation coproduct.", "reasoning": "Directly supported precursor chemistry: Shen et al. (1987) identified imidazole-4-acetaldehyde and reported a maximum yield of 1.6% relative to erythrose. Shen et al. (1990) subsequently obtained histidine after adding HCN and ammonia to the precursor-generating mixture. The aldehyde maximum and the later 3.5% histidine yield are different experimental observations and must not be multiplied as isolated sequential yields. Formamidine hydrolysis and erythrose decay remain central geochemical bottlenecks (V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018). No productive formamidine-supply reaction is invented here."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Strecker capture of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K; proposed capture window, not a recovered Shen experimental value", "pressure": "Near ambient, with gas-liquid retention or replenishment", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed staging at approximately pH 8-9 after the aldehyde-generating stage. HCN and ammonia must be retained or replenished because volatilization competes with capture. Their experimental concentrations and reaction duration are not supplied in the research summary and are not assigned invented values. Rock-covered aqueous compartments could attenuate surface UV but have not been experimentally coupled to this synthesis."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine or iminium equivalent with the aldehyde; cyanide addition installs the alpha-amino nitrile center. The imine is treated as a transient mechanistic species rather than a separately established accumulated intermediate.", "reasoning": "This is a mechanistic subdivision of the experimentally completed Strecker sequence reported by Shen et al. (1990), not an independently demonstrated isolation of the aminonitrile. Their multi-technique identification of histidine supports the overall aldehyde-mixture plus HCN/ammonia connection. The supplied research does not establish the proposed capture window, productive reagent ratios, residence time, or physical coupling to a natural pool; those remain validation requirements."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Hydrolysis of histidine aminonitrile to histidine", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions: acid-base catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed hydrolysis window, not a reported validated residence-time regime", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed separate acidic stage at approximately pH 1-2 after cyanide capture. Residual cyanide management and precursor survival must be established before acidification. The supplied Shen summary does not provide hydrolysis temperature, acidity, or duration, so no numerical residence time is claimed. Histidine and released ammonia are predominantly protonated under these conditions; neutral formulas are bookkeeping references."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile hydration followed by amide hydrolysis produces the carboxylic acid and releases the nitrile nitrogen as ammonia/ammonium. The transient amide is not asserted to have been isolated.", "reasoning": "Conventional hydrolysis closes the Strecker interpretation of the histidine synthesis demonstrated by Shen et al. (1990), whose overall histidine yield was 3.5% relative to erythrose. That endpoint does not independently validate this proposed mild-temperature acidic implementation. Hydrolysis rate, competing decomposition, and the attainable yield require measurement. Avoiding the hottest hydrothermal regimes is justified by the general amino-acid destruction observed by Ito et al. (2006), but those measurements are not histidine-specific rate constants."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Empirical histidine source: buffered discharge in a Teflon-only reactor", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005", "mol_013", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_012", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K receiving-water scenario; exact experimental reservoir temperature is not specified in the supplied report; spark plasma is nonthermal", "pressure": "Reported initial partial pressures: CH4 200 \u00b1 20 mbar, NH3 200 \u00b1 20 mbar, N2 100 \u00b1 20 mbar; water vapor additional", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported protocol: 200 mL water, approximately 30 kV discharge, 14 days elapsed with approximately seven days energized; approximately pH 8.7 with 0.05 M NH4Cl. Teflon-only reactor without borosilicate. Teflon is an experimental inert-wall control, not a proposed natural mineral. No added H2 or O2."}, "mechanism": "Unresolved gas-phase and aqueous organic chemistry followed by analytical recovery of a histidine-assigned product. No erythrose, imidazole aldehyde, Strecker, or hydantoin mechanism is assigned.", "reasoning": "Criado-Reyes et al. (2021) reported histidine in the buffered Teflon-only configuration, showing that borosilicate is not obligatory. Buffered configurations collectively yielded approximately 0.08-0.12 mg histidine per experiment; the supplied summary does not assign a specific value to this configuration. Identification used derivatized GC-MS library matching, and histidine was not among the explicitly co-injected authentic-standard confirmations. This is an empirical source term, not an elementary reaction. N2 is retained on both sides without asserting nitrogen provenance; H2 enables formal selected-product bookkeeping and is not a reported stoichiometric yield."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Empirical histidine source: buffered discharge in a borosilicate reactor", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005", "mol_013", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_012", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Borosilicate glass: experimental contact surface, not an established histidine-specific catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K receiving-water scenario; exact experimental reservoir temperature not specified in the supplied report; nonthermal discharge", "pressure": "Reported initial partial pressures: CH4 200 \u00b1 20 mbar, NH3 200 \u00b1 20 mbar, N2 100 \u00b1 20 mbar; water vapor additional", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported protocol: borosilicate reactor; 200 mL water; approximately pH 8.7 using 0.05 M NH4Cl; approximately 30 kV discharge; 14 days elapsed and approximately seven days energized. Laboratory glass is not equated with a naturally occurring mineral or a demonstrated natural reaction rate."}, "mechanism": "Empirical, unresolved discharge-driven formation and aqueous processing of a complex organic mixture containing the histidine-assigned endpoint.", "reasoning": "Histidine was reported in this configuration by Criado-Reyes et al. (2021). Glass affected the broader product network, but the paper does not establish a glass-catalyzed histidine mechanism. The approximately 0.08-0.12 mg range applies across buffered configurations, not a known configuration-specific value here. The GC-MS assignment and absence of target-specific co-injection require qualification. This is a reactor-surface variant of rxn_006, not independent evidence for a second molecular mechanism; gas coproduct bookkeeping is not a measured product balance."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Empirical histidine source: buffered discharge in Teflon with borosilicate pieces", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005", "mol_013", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_012", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Borosilicate glass pieces: experimental contact surfaces"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K receiving-water scenario; exact experimental reservoir temperature not specified in the supplied report; nonthermal discharge", "pressure": "Reported initial partial pressures: CH4 200 \u00b1 20 mbar, NH3 200 \u00b1 20 mbar, N2 100 \u00b1 20 mbar; water vapor additional", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported protocol: Teflon reactor containing borosilicate pieces; 200 mL water; approximately pH 8.7 with 0.05 M NH4Cl; approximately 30 kV discharge; 14 days elapsed with approximately seven days energized. No obligatory histidine-specific surface catalyst is inferred."}, "mechanism": "Unresolved discharge and aqueous processing represented only by the reported histidine-assigned endpoint.", "reasoning": "Criado-Reyes et al. (2021) included this surface-contact configuration and reported histidine across all reactor configurations. The buffered-group amount was approximately 0.08-0.12 mg per experiment; the individual amount is not specified in the supplied summary. Histidine-specific intermediates and nitrogen provenance were not measured, and the GC-MS identification warrants independent confirmation. This pathway tests surface contact within the same discharge family rather than adding an independently established synthesis mechanism."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Empirical histidine source: initially alkaline unbuffered discharge in Teflon", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005", "mol_013", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_012", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K receiving-water scenario; exact experimental reservoir temperature not specified in the supplied report; nonthermal discharge", "pressure": "Reported initial partial pressures: CH4 200 \u00b1 20 mbar, NH3 200 \u00b1 20 mbar, N2 100 \u00b1 20 mbar; water vapor additional", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported protocol: Teflon-only reactor; 200 mL water; unbuffered mixture initially approximately pH 11.1; approximately 30 kV discharge; 14 days elapsed with approximately seven days energized. The initial pH is not asserted to remain constant. No added H2 or O2."}, "mechanism": "Unresolved plasma-generated precursor chemistry and aqueous aging produce a histidine-assigned analytical endpoint.", "reasoning": "Criado-Reyes et al. (2021) reported histidine in unbuffered Teflon-only experiments. Across unbuffered configurations, reported amounts were approximately 0.20-0.31 mg per experiment, higher than the buffered-group range, but these ranges do not establish a histidine-specific rate law or pH mechanism. Analytical and mechanistic limitations remain the same as in the buffered experiments. The absence of borosilicate again supports using no obligatory mineral catalyst."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Empirical histidine source: initially alkaline unbuffered discharge in borosilicate", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005", "mol_013", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_012", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Borosilicate glass: experimental contact surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K receiving-water scenario; exact experimental reservoir temperature not specified in the supplied report; nonthermal discharge", "pressure": "Reported initial partial pressures: CH4 200 \u00b1 20 mbar, NH3 200 \u00b1 20 mbar, N2 100 \u00b1 20 mbar; water vapor additional", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported protocol: borosilicate reactor; 200 mL water; unbuffered initial pH approximately 11.1; approximately 30 kV discharge; 14 days elapsed with approximately seven days energized. No constant-pH trajectory or histidine-specific glass catalysis is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Coarse-grained discharge and liquid-phase reaction network with a reported histidine-assigned endpoint; individual target-forming transformations remain unknown.", "reasoning": "This configuration belongs to the unbuffered experiments reporting approximately 0.20-0.31 mg histidine across reactor types (Criado-Reyes et al., 2021). The supplied summary does not establish its individual yield. General effects of glass on organic chemistry cannot identify the histidine pathway. The GC-MS library assignment was not supplemented by the paper's target-specific authentic-standard co-injection subset. This is an operational variant, not an additional resolved mechanism."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Empirical histidine source: initially alkaline unbuffered discharge in Teflon with glass pieces", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005", "mol_013", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_012", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Borosilicate glass pieces: experimental contact surfaces"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K receiving-water scenario; exact experimental reservoir temperature not specified in the supplied report; nonth